.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library provides high-throughput, low-latency combinatorial optimization and
genetic search capabilities via a stable C-ABI.
It is engineered from first principles for zero runtime heap allocations
.Pq Ql Delta Heap(g) = 0
during generational iterations, maximum CPU L1 Data Cache residency, and
direct integration with host languages including C, C++, Rust, Python, and
Apple Swift.
.Pp
At initialization via
.Fn big_ll_create
or
.Fn big_ll_create_custom ,
the engine allocates two contiguous memory arenas representing the parent and
offspring populations.
Subsequent generational steps executed via
.Fn big_ll_step
or
.Fn big_ll_run
alternate pointers between these arenas, eliminating all calls to operating
system allocators
.Pq Fn malloc , Fn free
within active search loops.
.Sh DATA TYPES
.Bl -tag -width "big_ll_options_t"
.It Vt big_ll_handle_t
An opaque pointer to an internal evolutionary solver state machine.
Handles must be released by passing them to
.Fn big_ll_destroy .
.It Vt big_ll_eval_fn
A function pointer pointing to the user's objective evaluation routine:
.Pp
.Dl "double (*big_ll_eval_fn)(const uint64_t *genes, size_t word_count, void *user_data);"
.Pp
The callback accepts a pointer to the 64-bit word array of the candidate
chromosome, the number of 64-bit words
.Pq Ar word_count ,
and the user-supplied context pointer
.Pq Ar user_data .
The function must return an IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point fitness
value (higher values indicate superior fitness).
The callback must not mutate the memory pointed to by
.Ar genes .
.It Vt big_ll_options_t
A configuration structure passed to
.Fn big_ll_create_custom :
.Bl -tag -width "num_threads" -compact
.It Va enable_dedup
A boolean flag.
When set to true, the solver uses an internal 64-bit Robin Hood hash table
to detect identical candidate chromosomes and skip duplicate evaluations.
.It Va num_threads
The number of worker threads to spawn for parallel fitness evaluation.
Setting this to 0 defaults to the physical Performance core count detected
on the host SoC.
.El
.El
.Sh FUNCTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "big_ll_get_chromosome_fitness"
.It Fn big_ll_create "chromosome_bits" "pop_size" "seed" "eval_fn" "user_data"
Allocates and initializes an evolutionary solver instance.
.Ar chromosome_bits
must be greater than 0 and a multiple of 64.
.Ar pop_size
specifies the candidate count per generation.
.Ar seed
initializes the internal 64-bit Wyhash pseudo-random generator.
Returns an opaque handle on success, or
.Dv NULL
if parameters are invalid or memory allocation fails.
.It Fn big_ll_create_custom "chromosome_bits" "pop_size" "seed" "eval_fn" "user_data" "options"
Identical to
.Fn big_ll_create ,
but accepts fine-grained configuration flags via
.Vt big_ll_options_t .
.It Fn big_ll_step "handle"
Advances the evolutionary simulation by exactly one generation.
Returns 0 on success, or -1 if
.Ar handle
is invalid.
.It Fn big_ll_run "handle" "generations"
Advances the evolutionary simulation by
.Ar generations
consecutive iterations.
Returns 0 on success, or -1 if
.Ar handle
is invalid.
.It Fn big_ll_get_best_fitness "handle"
Returns the floating-point fitness score of the elite individual currently
discovered in the population.
.It Fn big_ll_get_best_genes "handle" "out_buffer" "buffer_words"
Copies the bitboard of the elite individual into
.Ar out_buffer .
At most
.Ar buffer_words
are copied.
Returns the actual number of 64-bit words written.
.It Fn big_ll_get_best_genes_ptr "handle"
Returns a direct, read-only pointer to the 64-bit word array of the elite
individual inside the active population arena.
This pointer remains valid until the next invocation of
.Fn big_ll_step ,
.Fn big_ll_run ,
or
.Fn big_ll_destroy .
.It Fn big_ll_get_generation "handle"
Returns the 0-indexed count of completed generations.
.It Fn big_ll_get_pop_size "handle"
Returns the active population size.
.It Fn big_ll_get_chromosome_bits "handle"
Returns the total bit-width of each candidate chromosome.
.It Fn big_ll_get_word_count "handle"
Returns the number of 64-bit words representing each chromosome
.Pq Ql word_count = chromosome_bits / 64 .
.It Fn big_ll_get_chromosome_words "handle" "index"
Returns a read-only pointer to the 64-bit word array for candidate individual
at
.Ar index .
.Ar index
must be strictly less than
.Fn big_ll_get_pop_size .
.It Fn big_ll_get_chromosome_fitness "handle" "index"
Returns the fitness score evaluated for candidate individual at
.Ar index .
.It Fn big_ll_destroy "handle"
Releases all memory arenas, thread pools, and resources associated with
.Ar handle .
Passing
.Dv NULL
is a safe no-op.
.El
.Sh SWIFT INTEGRATION
Apple Swift projects can consume
.Nm
directly using Swift Package Manager.
The Swift module
.Nm BigLL
wraps the C-ABI in an idiomatic, ARC-managed class
.Vt BigLLSolver :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
import BigLL

// Define an objective function via Swift closure
let solver = BigLLSolver(chromosomeBits: 256, populationSize: 128) { words in
    var ones = 0
    for word in words {
        ones += word.nonzeroBitCount
    }
    return Double(ones)
}

// Run 100 generations
solver.run(generations: 100)

let bestScore = solver.bestFitness
print("Elite Fitness: ", bestScore)
.Ed
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Distinct
.Vt big_ll_handle_t
instances are fully isolated and may be executed concurrently across different
threads without mutual locking.
However, a single
.Vt big_ll_handle_t
is not re-entrant: concurrent calls to
.Fn big_ll_step
or accessor routines on the same handle from multiple threads without external
synchronization invoke undefined behavior.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following complete C99 program optimizes a 256-bit OneMax problem
using
.Nm :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include "big_ll.h"

static double evaluate_onemax(
    const uint64_t *genes,
    size_t word_count,
    void *user_data
) {
    (void)user_data;
    double count = 0.0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < word_count; ++i) {
        count += (double)__builtin_popcountll(genes[i]);
    }
    return count;
}

int main(void) {
    big_ll_handle_t solver = big_ll_create(
        256, 128, 42, evaluate_onemax, NULL
    );
    if (!solver) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to initialize solver\en");
        return 1;
    }

    big_ll_run(solver, 100);

    double elite_fit = big_ll_get_best_fitness(solver);
    printf("Optimal fitness found: %.1f / 256.0\en", elite_fit);

    big_ll_destroy(solver);
    return 0;
}
